xnu 0383: wfile buffer is neither contiguous nor permanent

A 22 MB wfile left the phone unable to answer anything but pings: the
handler allocates the file size (0381) and the grow path frees the
previous buffer, kmem_alloc_contig waited for a gap of that size in a
fragmented kernel map, and the old buffer carried KMA_PERMANENT, which
vm_map_delete refuses to remove. Measured 2026-11-19: the 21.5 MB first
upload of a boot arrived whole, the 22 MB one that followed panicked on
the screen. Plain kmem_alloc without KMA_PERMANENT does both jobs; the
buffer is only copied into and given to the VFS.

--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_usb.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_usb.c
@@ -2388,9 +2388,9 @@
 	}
 	/*
 	 * The buffer is the file's size, not the ceiling: an 11.5 MB dylib is a
-	 * normal upload here (Turnip), and a 32 MB contiguous allocation for a
-	 * 66 KB program would fail on a fragmented kernel map. It grows, and is
-	 * never given back: the next large upload reuses it.
+	 * normal upload here (Turnip), and a 32 MB buffer for a 66 KB program
+	 * would waste the kernel map. It grows, and is never given back: the
+	 * next large upload reuses it.
 	 */
 	if ((vm_size_t)len > ud_wfile_cap) {
 		vm_offset_t buf = 0;
@@ -2402,8 +2402,18 @@
 			ud_wfile_buf = NULL;
 			ud_wfile_cap = 0;
 		}
-		if (kmem_alloc_contig(kernel_map, &buf, need, PAGE_MASK,
-		    0, 0, KMA_DATA | KMA_ZERO | KMA_PERMANENT,
+		/*
+		 * Plain kmem_alloc, and not permanent. The buffer is only
+		 * copied into and handed to the VFS, so no run of pages is
+		 * needed, and kmem_alloc_contig waits for a gap that size in a
+		 * kernel map iOS has fragmented. KMA_PERMANENT must not be
+		 * set: the grow below frees the old buffer, and vm_map_delete
+		 * panics on a permanent entry ("Attempting to remove permanent
+		 * VM map entry", measured on the phone 2026-11-19 when a 22 MB
+		 * upload followed the 21.5 MB one in the same boot).
+		 */
+		if (kmem_alloc(kernel_map, &buf, need,
+		    KMA_DATA | KMA_ZERO,
 		    VM_KERN_MEMORY_IOKIT) != KERN_SUCCESS) {
 			n = snprintf(out, sizeof(out), "wfile has no memory\n");
 			ud_cmd_send(so, to, out, (size_t)n);
